d by basic common sense  now about quantitative easing let's talk  about what that is economically first  that was the Fed borrowing four trillion  from banks and proceeding to buy up US  Treasuries with an eye on influencing  long rates of interest downward and also  buying up mortgages to stimulate the  housing market so let's get one thing  out of the way right here quantitative  easing had nothing to do with economic  as a rule it weakened the economy and  that's the case because governments once  again can't spend us to prosperity they  can only miss allocate the wealth we've  already created as for housing it is not  a driver of economic activity when you  buy a house your purchase of a house  doesn't make you more productive it  doesn't open up foreign markets for you  it doesn't lead to cancer here's the the  elongate life it doesn't lead to  software innovations that make us more  productive it doesn't expand time  housing is consumption that reduces the  amount of capital available for  entrepreneurs and businesses to expand  and securely on its face was an economic  depressant not a driver of growth at  which point to believe that it  stimulated a stock market boom you have  to believe that the deepest markets in  the world that is the US markets  populated by the most sophisticated  investors in the world were tricked by  something so plainly and nimma chol to  economic growth I think the very idea  offends what we're all about the idea  that these guys could trick the smartest  markets in the world let's be serious  but I get all the responses and I one of  my favorite ones came from an economist  in Austin Texas he said oh no no no  Johnny you're not an economist see you  don't understand the Fed and conducting  quantitative easing signaled to  investors that we've got your back that  if you buy equities will protect you  we'll keep those markets propped up and  that's what caused the stock market  rally and he added I learned this from a  Nobel laurea