ad nowhere to go but this  stock market and I say well didn't  really create four trillion but if you  want to believe that that's fine but for  4 trillion to enter the stock market by  definition for trillion must exit in  this netherworld inhabited by economists  and academics there are only buyers but  in the real world for a buyer to express  optimism a seller must be able to  express an equal amount of pessimism for  a QE excited optimist to express that  view in the stock market as a rule a QE  skeptic must get to express an equal  amount of pessimism in markets they are  always in everywhere  related buyers and buy buyers and  sellers for for a bull to be optimistic  in the market a bear must express an  equal amount of pessimism these things  equal each other out at which point we  get to the basic truth about markets  themselves they quite simply do not  price in the present they always price  in the future well the feds official  program of quantitative easing ended in  2014 but a telegraphed its end as early  as 2011 so if in fact QE had been the  driver of this stock market rally logic  dictates that stocks would have  corrected and corrected for good long  before 2014 to reflect the feds eventual  end of the program but in fact stocks  have continued to reach new highs  there's quite simply no evidence despite  what we're told that the Fed created a  stock market rally but there are lots of  people who believe that most economists  will tell you that most of cons will say  he's not what he doesn't know what he's  talking about you can read Morgan  Stanley's Russia or Sharma he regularly  puts op-eds into the Wall Street Journal  saying oh no we on Wall Street realized  this stock market boom was just a  creation of the Fed and so you're  welcome to that point of view I fully  agree that I am on the outside on this  making a different argument but if you  believe that if you believe the Fed  despite all evidence and logic was the  source of a major stock